menu "Optical Flow Configuration"

    choice OPTFLOW_BOARD
        prompt "Camera board"
        default OPTFLOW_BOARD_S3_WROOM_CAM if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
        default OPTFLOW_BOARD_AI_THINKER
        help
            Selects the pin map in camera_pins.h.

        config OPTFLOW_BOARD_AI_THINKER
            bool "AI-Thinker ESP32-CAM"
            depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32
        config OPTFLOW_BOARD_S3_WROOM_CAM
            bool "ESP32-S3-WROOM CAM (Freenove pin map, OV3660)"
            depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
            help
                The ESP32-S3-WROOM CAM board with the Freenove / ESP32-S3-EYE
                camera pin map (docs/s3-wroom-cam-wiring-plan.md), fitted with
                an OV3660. The OV2640 also works on it; the OV3660 frame timing
                below applies only when the sensor reports PID 0x3660.
        config OPTFLOW_BOARD_ESP_EYE
            bool "ESP-EYE"
        config OPTFLOW_BOARD_XIAO_S3
            bool "Seeed XIAO ESP32S3 Sense"
    endchoice

    choice OPTFLOW_FRAME
        prompt "Frame size (grayscale)"
        default OPTFLOW_FRAME_QVGA if OPTFLOW_BOARD_S3_WROOM_CAM
        default OPTFLOW_FRAME_QQVGA
        help
            The matcher's cost follows grid^2 x (2*search+1)^2 x block^2, and
            the search radius must grow with the frame width to keep the same
            velocity range, so the frame size is decided by the match time,
            not by the camera or the memory. host/size_bench (make bench) and
            OPTFLOW_BENCH measure it; docs/esp32-cam-optical-flow-report.md
            section 13 has the table. QVGA with a 4x4 grid is the largest that
            keeps 80 ms with margin by that table; CIF fits only on paper.

        config OPTFLOW_FRAME_QQVGA
            bool "QQVGA 160x120"
        config OPTFLOW_FRAME_HQVGA
            bool "HQVGA 240x176"
        config OPTFLOW_FRAME_QVGA
            bool "QVGA 320x240"
        config OPTFLOW_FRAME_CIF
            bool "CIF 400x296"
    endchoice

    config OPTFLOW_S3_UART
        bool "Copy the CSV rows to the flight board on UART1"
        depends on OPTFLOW_BOARD_AI_THINKER || OPTFLOW_BOARD_S3_WROOM_CAM
        default y
        help
            Every CSV row also goes out on UART1 for the S3-Zero to read and
            log (its cam_link.c tells these rows from target_cam's by their 16
            columns). Only data rows; the 's' command stops the PC copy only.
            Nothing reads RX. Pins are in camera_pins.h: TX IO14 and RX IO13 on
            the AI-Thinker (target_cam's wiring); TX IO14 and RX IO21 on the
            S3-WROOM CAM, whose IO13 is the camera's PCLK.

    config OPTFLOW_S3_UART_BAUD
        int "Flight-board UART baud rate"
        depends on OPTFLOW_S3_UART
        range 9600 2000000
        default 115200
        help
            The S3 reads its camera UART at 115200. A ~90-byte row takes about
            8 ms on the wire, well within the 80 ms frame period.

    config OPTFLOW_FRAME_PERIOD_MS
        int "OV3660 frame period (ms, 0 = the driver's own timing)"
        range 0 200
        default 80
        help
            With an OV3660, set the sensor's own frame length so that it
            delivers one frame every this many milliseconds; the loop then
            takes every frame, as it does on the AI-Thinker, where the OV2640
            at XCLK 20 MHz happens to run at 79.95 ms. The esp32-camera driver
            clocks the OV3660 at SYSCLK = XCLK for QVGA to HVGA in grayscale,
            which by its own timing gives about 90 ms at XCLK 20 MHz; this
            raises SYSCLK to 40 MHz (it needs an XCLK that divides 160 MHz)
            and stretches the vertical blanking to the period. Reasoned from
            the driver's PLL formula, not measured: the boot banner prints the
            period actually measured. Ignored for other sensors.

    config OPTFLOW_TARGET_FPS
        int "Target frame rate (frames per second)"
        range 1 30
        default 15
        help
            Frames are paced to this rate deliberately. Optical flow for a blimp
            needs nothing faster, and running the sensor slower is the single
            most effective way to keep an ESP32-CAM cool. Changeable at runtime
            with the 'f' command.

    config OPTFLOW_XCLK_MHZ
        int "Sensor clock XCLK (MHz)"
        range 4 20
        default 10
        help
            The OV2640 pixel pipeline is clocked from this. 20 MHz is the usual
            example value; 10 MHz halves the sensor's own dissipation and is
            still far more bandwidth than a paced 15 FPS QQVGA stream needs.
            Below about 6 MHz some OV2640 modules fail to produce frames.

    config OPTFLOW_HEIGHT_MM
        int "Default height above the floor (mm)"
        range 100 10000
        default 2000
        help
            Used to convert pixel flow into millimetres per second. Pixel flow
            scales with 1/height, so this number must be right - or supplied by
            a rangefinder in flight. Changeable at runtime with the 'h' command.

    config OPTFLOW_HFOV_DEG
        int "Horizontal field of view (degrees)"
        range 10 150
        default 54
        help
            Horizontal FOV of the lens at the configured frame size. 54 degrees
            is the computed horizontal component of the stock OV2640 65-degree
            diagonal lens at 4:3. CALIBRATE THIS: it is a direct scale factor on
            every velocity this firmware reports.

    config OPTFLOW_BLOCK
        int "Match block size (pixels)"
        range 8 32
        default 16

    config OPTFLOW_SEARCH
        int "Search radius (pixels)"
        range 1 10
        default 10 if OPTFLOW_FRAME_CIF
        default 8 if OPTFLOW_FRAME_QVGA
        default 7
        help
            Bounds the largest measurable motion to search - 1 px per frame:
            at 2 m, 12.5 FPS and a 58 degree lens, 7 at QQVGA is about
            +/-1.0 m/s, 8 at QVGA about +/-0.6 m/s. Larger costs cycles
            quadratically.

    config OPTFLOW_GRID
        int "Grid of match blocks (N x N)"
        range 1 8
        default 4 if OPTFLOW_FRAME_QVGA || OPTFLOW_FRAME_CIF
        default 5
        help
            N*N blocks are matched per frame and the median is reported, so a
            person walking underneath cannot drag the estimate with them.

    config OPTFLOW_MIN_VAR
        int "Minimum block variance to attempt a match"
        range 0 10000
        default 50
        help
            A featureless floor patch has no recoverable motion. Blocks below
            this are reported as rejected rather than matched to noise.

    config OPTFLOW_BENCH
        bool "Run the frame-size benchmark at boot"
        default n
        help
            Before the camera starts, time flow_match on synthetic frames at
            every candidate frame size (main/flow_bench.c, the table host/
            size_bench prints) with the frames in internal RAM and in PSRAM,
            print the table, then carry on as usual. It needs no camera, so
            it runs on any board of the target. About 30 s at boot.

endmenu
